#sg13g2 - a starter design-rule deck for GDS Viewer.
#
#NOT A SIGNOFF DECK, AND NOT AFFILIATED WITH IHP. This is a hand-transcribed subset for catching obvious mistakes
#while looking at a layout. A design is signed off against the PDK's own decks - KLayout's in
#ihp-sg13g2/libs.tech/klayout/tech/drc - not against this.
#
#Layer numbers are from the layer table of the SG13G2 layout rules, and every id, value and description from their
#rule tables - SG13G2_os_layout_rules.pdf Rev. 0.4, and the same tables as text in
#ihp-sg13g2/libs.tech/klayout/tech/drc/docs, main_rules.md and extra_rules.md, in github.com/IHP-GmbH/IHP-Open-PDK at
#5e6d592. Both rule tables are vendored at OtherResources/IHP, where DrcDeckTests holds every rule here to them.
#
#THREE THINGS TO KNOW BEFORE TRUSTING A RESULT HERE
#
#**The layers IHP allows at 45 degrees are measured as distance, as IHP's rules measure them.** SG13G2 draws Activ,
#GatPoly, Metal1 to Metal5 and the two top metals at 135 degrees as well as 90 (rules 3.1) - its own standard cells
#turn their poly with stairs of 5 nm steps. Measured as squares, which is what a rule naming no metric gets, a
#45-degree line reads 1.41 times nearer its neighbor than it is: sg13g2_tielo's two poly pieces are 182 nm apart,
#which Gat.b's 180 allows, and 129 as squares - and a Tiny Tapeout tile built of the same cells reported 587 such
#faults. So the width and space of those nine layers name `euclidean`, which measures a diagonal line as what it is
#and two corners approaching each other diagonally as well. Everything else - the wells, the implants, the contacts
#and vias, which IHP keeps at 90 degrees, MIM and Passiv - is measured as squares, which is exact on a layer with no
#diagonals.
#
#**The edge engine `euclidean` runs on matches KLayout over IHP's layouts.** It reported faults that were not there
#until 2026-11-02 on layouts whose metal overlaps itself - one M3.a inside a 60 GHz amplifier core's 1950 nm wide
#Metal3, M1.a in a TIA's and a bandgap's metal, tens of thousands more measured as distance - and these nine layers
#were measured `parallel`, between facing edges only, until it was fixed. Over IHP's 84 cells and the amplifier,
#TIA, bandgap and two SRAM macros it now reports what KLayout reports, which is nothing. A width or space fault on
#these layers that the drawing does not show is still worth measuring with the ruler before believing.
#
#Values are DATABASE UNITS, which for SG13G2 is nanometers: the micron figure in the rules times 1000. Areas are
#square nanometers, so 0.144 um2 is 144000. Nothing is scaled and nothing is rounded.

#Drawn layers ***********************************************************************

layer NWell        31/0
layer ThickGateOx  44/0
layer Activ         1/0
layer GatPoly       5/0
layer pSD          14/0
layer SalBlock     28/0
layer Cont          6/0
layer Metal1        8/0
layer Via1         19/0
layer Metal2       10/0
layer Via2         29/0
layer Metal3       30/0
layer Via3         49/0
layer Metal4       50/0
layer Via4         66/0
layer Metal5       67/0
layer MIM          36/0
layer TopVia1     125/0
layer TopMetal1   126/0
layer TopVia2     133/0
layer TopMetal2   134/0
layer Passiv        9/0

#Derived layers *********************************************************************
#
#A TopVia1 that lands on the MIM's top plate rather than on Metal5. MIM.d is the plate's enclosure of it, and holds
#only for those: a TopVia1 elsewhere is not on a MIM at all.

derive mimvia = TopVia1 and MIM

#Rules ******************************************************************************
#
#id         check      operands               value   modifiers  description
#
#NWell. A space under the same-net limit is always wrong - the rules merge the two - so that is the limit checked;
#two wells on different nets need 1.8, which only a netlist can say.
rule NW.a       width      NWell                 620              "Min. NWell width"
rule NW.b       space      NWell                 620              "Min. NWell space or notch (same net)"

#The thick gate oxide
rule TGO.e      space      ThickGateOx           860              "Min. ThickGateOx space"
rule TGO.f      width      ThickGateOx           860              "Min. ThickGateOx width"

#Activ. Act.e's "enclosed area" is the field Activ closes round, which is a hole in the merged layer.
rule Act.a      width      Activ                 150   euclidean  "Min. Activ width"
rule Act.b      space      Activ                 210   euclidean  "Min. Activ space or notch"
rule Act.d      area       Activ              122000              "Min. Activ area"
rule Act.e      holearea   Activ              150000              "Min. Activ enclosed area"

#GatPoly
rule Gat.a      width      GatPoly               130   euclidean  "Min. GatPoly width"
rule Gat.b      space      GatPoly               180   euclidean  "Min. GatPoly space or notch"
rule Gat.e      area       GatPoly             90000              "Min. GatPoly area"

#The p+ implant and the salicide block
rule pSD.a      width      pSD                   310              "Min. pSD width"
rule pSD.b      space      pSD                   310              "Min. pSD space or notch"
rule Sal.a      width      SalBlock              420              "Min. SalBlock width"
rule Sal.b      space      SalBlock              420              "Min. SalBlock space or notch"

#Cont. Cnt.a is a minimum and a maximum - every contact is 0.16 square - and only the minimum is checked here. Its
#enclosures are in the list at the foot of this file.
rule Cnt.a      width      Cont                  160              "Min. Cont width"
rule Cnt.b      space      Cont                  180              "Min. Cont space"

#Metal1 to Metal5. A metal's c rule is the least it covers the via below it by.
rule M1.a       width      Metal1                160   euclidean  "Min. Metal1 width"
rule M1.b       space      Metal1                180   euclidean  "Min. Metal1 space or notch"
rule M1.d       area       Metal1              90000              "Min. Metal1 area"
rule M2.a       width      Metal2                200   euclidean  "Min. Metal2 width"
rule M2.b       space      Metal2                210   euclidean  "Min. Metal2 space or notch"
rule M2.c       enclosure  Via1     Metal2         5              "Min. Metal2 enclosure of Via1"
rule M2.d       area       Metal2             144000              "Min. Metal2 area"
rule M3.a       width      Metal3                200   euclidean  "Min. Metal3 width"
rule M3.b       space      Metal3                210   euclidean  "Min. Metal3 space or notch"
rule M3.c       enclosure  Via2     Metal3         5              "Min. Metal3 enclosure of Via2"
rule M3.d       area       Metal3             144000              "Min. Metal3 area"
rule M4.a       width      Metal4                200   euclidean  "Min. Metal4 width"
rule M4.b       space      Metal4                210   euclidean  "Min. Metal4 space or notch"
rule M4.c       enclosure  Via3     Metal4         5              "Min. Metal4 enclosure of Via3"
rule M4.d       area       Metal4             144000              "Min. Metal4 area"
rule M5.a       width      Metal5                200   euclidean  "Min. Metal5 width"
rule M5.b       space      Metal5                210   euclidean  "Min. Metal5 space or notch"
rule M5.c       enclosure  Via4     Metal5         5              "Min. Metal5 enclosure of Via4"
rule M5.d       area       Metal5             144000              "Min. Metal5 area"

#Vias. Each a is a minimum and a maximum, as Cnt.a is, and only the minimum is checked. A via's c rule is the least
#the metal under it covers it by.
rule V1.a       width      Via1                  190              "Min. Via1 width"
rule V1.b       space      Via1                  220              "Min. Via1 space"
rule V1.c       enclosure  Via1     Metal1        10              "Min. Metal1 enclosure of Via1"
rule V2.a       width      Via2                  190              "Min. Via2 width"
rule V2.b       space      Via2                  220              "Min. Via2 space"
rule V2.c       enclosure  Via2     Metal2         5              "Min. Metal2 enclosure of Via2"
rule V3.a       width      Via3                  190              "Min. Via3 width"
rule V3.b       space      Via3                  220              "Min. Via3 space"
rule V3.c       enclosure  Via3     Metal3         5              "Min. Metal3 enclosure of Via3"
rule V4.a       width      Via4                  190              "Min. Via4 width"
rule V4.b       space      Via4                  220              "Min. Via4 space"
rule V4.c       enclosure  Via4     Metal4         5              "Min. Metal4 enclosure of Via4"

#The top vias and the two thick top metals
rule TV1.a      width      TopVia1               420              "Min. TopVia1 width"
rule TV1.b      space      TopVia1               420              "Min. TopVia1 space"
rule TV1.c      enclosure  TopVia1  Metal5       100              "Min. Metal5 enclosure of TopVia1"
rule TV1.d      enclosure  TopVia1  TopMetal1    420              "Min. TopMetal1 enclosure of TopVia1"
rule TM1.a      width      TopMetal1            1640   euclidean  "Min. TopMetal1 width"
rule TM1.b      space      TopMetal1            1640   euclidean  "Min. TopMetal1 space or notch"
rule TV2.a      width      TopVia2               900              "Min. TopVia2 width"
rule TV2.b      space      TopVia2              1060              "Min. TopVia2 space"
rule TV2.c      enclosure  TopVia2  TopMetal1    500              "Min. TopMetal1 enclosure of TopVia2"
rule TV2.d      enclosure  TopVia2  TopMetal2    500              "Min. TopMetal2 enclosure of TopVia2"
rule TM2.a      width      TopMetal2            2000   euclidean  "Min. TopMetal2 width"
rule TM2.b      space      TopMetal2            2000   euclidean  "Min. TopMetal2 space or notch"

#The MIM capacitor, between Metal5 and TopMetal1
rule MIM.a      width      MIM                  1140              "Min. MIM width"
rule MIM.b      space      MIM                   600              "Min. MIM space"
rule MIM.c      enclosure  MIM      Metal5       600              "Min. Metal5 enclosure of MIM"
rule MIM.d      enclosure  mimvia   MIM          360              "Min. MIM enclosure of TopVia1"
rule MIM.f      area       MIM               1300000              "Min. MIM area per MIM device"

#The passivation openings
rule Pas.a      width      Passiv               2100              "Min. Passiv width"
rule Pas.b      space      Passiv               3500              "Min. Passiv space or notch"

#Every coordinate against the manufacturing grid: "All features are on a drawing grid of 5 nm" (rules 3.1), and the
#off-grid checks of the PDK's own KLayout deck. Stated rather than read back off the file, for the reason sky130A.drc
#gives.
rule grid.1     offgrid    *                       5              "Coordinate off the 5 nm grid"

#Rules deliberately NOT here *********************************************************
#
#Real SG13G2 rules this checker cannot express, or that are not transcribed yet - listed so their absence is a
#decision on the record rather than an oversight.
#
#Cnt.c    Activ over a contact, 0.07, and
#Cnt.d    GatPoly over a contact, 0.07. Run over IHP's 84 standard cells and taken out for what they reported there:
#         seventeen cells failing at inside corners of the Activ, and tielo at its poly. The geometry is fine -
#         measured as distance, every one of those contacts is enclosed by 70 nm or more, most by exactly 70. An
#         enclosure here is measured by growing the contact as a square, and the square's corner reaches into the
#         notch; the edge engine measures as distance, but width and spacing only. The same two rules left
#         gf180mcuD.drc for the same reason, as CO.3 and CO.4. IHP also relaxes Cnt.c to 0.05 inside DigiBnd and to
#         0.006 inside SRAM.
#Cnt.e    A contact on GatPoly to Activ, 0.14, and
#Cnt.f    a contact on Activ to GatPoly, 0.11. Spacings between two layers, which this checker measures as squares
#         only - the metric words are refused on a two-layer space - and so would report the diagonal approaches
#         Cnt.c and Cnt.d did.
#Gat.d    GatPoly to Activ, 0.07. The same two-layer square measure would report every transistor, at the corners
#         where its gate crosses the Activ.
#Gat.a1   Gate lengths - Gat.a1 to Gat.a4 - and Gat.c, GatPoly beyond Activ, 0.18. Each is along one direction only,
#         and a width check of the gate would report every narrow transistor. DIRECTIONAL, like poly.7 and poly.8 in
#         sky130A.drc.
#M1.c     Metal1 over a contact. The least the rules allow is 0.00, which measures nothing, so the deck refuses it.
#M1.c1    The 0.05 asked for at a line's end over a contact, and V1.c1 and Mn.c1 over a via - each depends on finding
#         the end, which a region check cannot do.
#NW.b1    Two NWells on different nets, 1.8 - nets are not known here. The same for every "different net" rule.
#NW.c     NWell over P+Activ and NWell to N+Activ, NW.c to NW.f: each depends on which Activ is a tie and which is
#         inside ThickGateOx.
#NBL.a    nBuLay and PWell:block. Their rules are on the layers chapter 4.2 derives - nBuLay grown from every NWell
#         3.0 um wide or more, PWell from what no NWell covers - which this checker does not derive.
#M1.e     Wide-metal spacings, M1.e and M1.f and the same for Metal2 to TopMetal2: each holds only where a line is
#         wider than a limit and runs alongside another for more than a length.
#M1.g     The 45-degree rules, M1.g and M1.i and theirs above: they hold only on bent metal.
#M1.j     The density rules, of each metal and of fill: global, over the whole chip, so a cell or a tile would fail
#         them however it was drawn.
#Pas.c    TopMetal2 over a passivation opening, 2.1 - except inside the seal ring, which this cannot tell.
#MIM.e    TopMetal1 to MIM, 0.6, and MIM.g, the most a MIM may cover: a maximum is not a check here.
#Ant.a    The antenna ratios: they need each layer's role, and this deck is read without them.
#3.1      Acute angles under 87 degrees, and contacts and vias at anything but 90: angles are not a check here.
#3.2      The forbidden layers - BiWind, BasPoly, EmPoly, NoDRC and the rest - whose layout IHP rejects at tape-in:
#         that a layer is drawn on at all is not a check here either. The layer panel names none of them, which is
#         where one shows.
#
#And chapter 6's device rules - the bipolar transistors, the resistors, isolated NMOS, the Schottky diode, ESD, the
#pads, the seal ring and the inductors - which are each a device's own.
